  "body": "Recently, I was asked to code the following pattern for a client site: person's photo, name & job title of person, a quote by the person about the product. I wanted to use [figure img  figcaption../figcaption blockquote../blockquote /figure].\r\n\r\nHowever, that won't validate as the spec says figcaption must be \"the first or last child of a figure element\". Why the restriction? If the design were [name & job, img, blockquote] or [img, blockquote, name] I could use figure. Of course, I could order my content so that a legal structure were used, then change the visual layout with CSS display:table hacks or flexbox, but why should this be necessary?\r\n\r\nSurely, any UA that needs unambiguously to find a caption can do so from a dedicated element anywhere within a figure? \r\n\r\n",
